  <h2>Personal memory</h2>
  <p>A calendar remembers the meeting. A tracker remembers the walk. Memoato keeps the context around them: what you agreed, why your plans changed, or the small detail you will need months later.</p>
  <p>Write in your own words. The original note stays connected to the useful details extracted from it, so you can review an interpretation and return to the source.</p>
  <p><a href="/#how-it-works">See personal memory in practice</a></p>

  <h2>Context for AI-assisted work</h2>
  <p>Project knowledge changes across documents, issues, and code. An AI answer is more useful when you can check which source supports it, whether it is still current, and who can use it.</p>
  <p>The Context pilot brings read-only GitHub and Linear sources into a single-owner workspace. A person reviews claims before they are available for retrieval. Access grants and freshness are checked before building a context packet.</p>
  <p><a href="/#ai-context">Explore the Context pilot</a></p>

  <h2>One principle behind both</h2>
  <p>Keep the source distinct from the interpretation. A saved note or source version is evidence; an extracted fact is something to review. This is how Memoato makes memory useful without hiding where an answer came from.</p>

  <h2>Built openly</h2>
  <p>Memoato is an open-source project from <a href="https://hills-lab.hr" rel="noopener noreferrer">HILLS Lab</a>, created by <a href="https://hrvoje.pavlinovic.com" rel="noopener noreferrer">Hrvoje Pavlinovic</a>. It grows out of everyday use and hands-on engineering work.</p>
  <p>The personal web app and MCP connection are available today. Project context is a pilot with one owner per workspace. Read <a href="/help">how to get started</a> and <a href="/privacy-first">how storage and AI processing differ</a>.</p>

